3. CONCEPT EXPLANATION
3.1 What is an operator?
Definition. A symbol that performs an operation on one or more values (operands) and produces a result.
Unary: one operand: -x, !flag, typeof x, x++
Binary: two operands: a + b, a > b
Ternary: three operands: cond ? a : b (the only one)
Expression. Any piece of code that produces a value: 5, a + b, a > b, x = 10 (yes, assignment is an expression producing the assigned value). Output prediction is largely "evaluate the expression, then print the value".
3.2 Arithmetic operators
Operator	Name	Example	Result
+	Addition	7 + 2	9
-	Subtraction	7 - 2	5
*	Multiplication	7 * 2	14
/	Division	7 / 2	3.5
%	Remainder (modulo)	7 % 2	1
**	Exponentiation	7 ** 2	49
Remainder %, the most interview-relevant. a % b is what is left after dividing a by b as many whole times as possible.
javascript
console.log(10 % 3);   // 1   (3 goes in 3 times = 9, remainder 1)console.log(10 % 5);   // 0   (divides exactly)console.log(3 % 10);   // 3   (10 goes in 0 times, remainder 3)console.log(-7 % 3);   // -1  (sign follows the left operand)console.log(7.5 % 2);  // 1.5 (works with decimals)
Why % matters. Even/odd (n % 2 === 0), divisibility (n % 5 === 0), extracting the last digit (n % 10), cycling through positions (i % length), time conversions (seconds % 60). Five of the nine Day 7 classic problems use it.
Division. Always produces a decimal when needed; no integer division. 7 / 2 is 3.5. Integer part: Math.floor(7 / 2) → 3 (introduce Math.floor as a supporting tool; needed for reverse-number on Day 7).
javascript
console.log(Math.floor(7 / 2));   // 3console.log(Math.floor(-7 / 2));  // -4  (rounds down, toward -∞)
+ with strings. Joins them (Day 1). Mixed string + number is coercion (Day 5). Today: keep + operands the same type.
Unary minus / plus. -x negates. +x converts to a number (Day 5).
Common mistakes. Expecting % to be "percentage"; using ^ for power (^ is bitwise XOR: 2 ^ 3 is 1, not 8); forgetting operator precedence.
3.3 Assignment operators
= stores the right value in the left variable. Compound forms do the operation then store.
javascript
let x = 10;x += 5;    // x = x + 5   → 15x -= 3;    // x = x - 3   → 12x *= 2;    // x = x * 2   → 24x /= 4;    // x = x / 4   → 6x %= 4;    // x = x % 4   → 2x **= 3;   // x = x ** 3  → 8console.log(x);   // 8
Important points.
Assignment is an expression: let a; let b = (a = 5); sets both to 5. Rarely good style, but appears in output questions.
= is assignment; === is comparison. Writing = where you meant === is the classic bug of Day 6.
Compound assignment evaluates the right side fully first: x *= 2 + 3 means x = x * (2 + 3), not x * 2 + 3.




3.4 Comparison operators
Every comparison produces a boolean.
Operator	Meaning	5 ? 3
 >	greater than	true
<	less than	false
>=	greater or equal	true
<=	less or equal	false
===	strictly equal (same type and value)	false
!==	strictly not equal	true
==	loosely equal (converts types first)	Day 5
!=	loosely not equal	Day 5
javascript
console.log(5 === 5);       // trueconsole.log(5 === "5");     // false  (different types)console.log("a" === "a");   // trueconsole.log(5 !== 4);       // trueconsole.log(10 >= 10);      // true
Rule for this course. Always use === and !==. == performs type conversion before comparing, which is the subject of Day 5; until then it is only mentioned.
Chained comparison does not work. 1 < x < 10 does not mean "x between 1 and 10". It evaluates (1 < x) to a boolean first, and then compares that boolean to 10 (Day 5 explains the conversion). Write x > 1 && x < 10.
Comparing strings. "apple" < "banana" is true: strings compare character by character by Unicode value. "Zebra" < "apple" is true because uppercase letters come before lowercase. "10" < "9" is true (string comparison, "1" < "9"), a classic trap.
Interview angle. "Difference between == and ===?" is asked in almost every JavaScript interview; today give the one-line answer ("=== checks type and value without conversion; == converts first") and promise the full picture tomorrow.



3.5 Logical operators (with booleans)
Operator	Name	Result
&&	AND	true only if both are true
||	OR	true if at least one is true
!	NOT	flips true ↔ false
javascript
let isAdult = true;let hasTicket = false;console.log(isAdult && hasTicket);   // falseconsole.log(isAdult || hasTicket);   // trueconsole.log(!isAdult);               // falseconsole.log(!hasTicket);             // true
Truth tables (teach once, test often).
A	B	A && B	A || B
true	true	true	true
true	false	false	true
false	true	false	true
false	false	false	false
Combining with comparisons.
javascript
let age = 25;let hasId = true;console.log(age >= 18 && hasId);            // trueconsole.log(age < 18 || age > 60);          // falseconsole.log(!(age >= 18));                  // false
Short-circuit evaluation (boolean version). && stops at the first false; || stops at the first true. With booleans this only affects performance; with non-boolean values it changes the result (Day 5).
Common mistakes. Writing age >= 18 && <= 60 (missing left operand → SyntaxError); confusing && with || in "between" checks; !a === b is (!a) === b, not !(a === b).
3.6 Increment and decrement
x++ and x-- add/subtract 1. The position matters when the expression's value is used.
javascript
let a = 5;let b = a++;   // postfix: b gets the OLD value (5), then a becomes 6console.log(a, b);   // 6 5let c = 5;let d = ++c;   // prefix: c becomes 6 FIRST, then d gets the NEW value (6)console.log(c, d);   // 6 6
Rule. Postfix x++: use, then increment. Prefix ++x: increment, then use. As a standalone statement (x++;), both do the same thing.
Interview angle. let i = 1; console.log(i++ + ++i); is a common trick question (answer in Section 6). Advice to give: in real code, write x++ only as a standalone statement; never embed it in a larger expression.
3.7 Ternary operator
Definition. condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse. A compact expression that chooses between two values.
javascript
let age = 20;let status = age >= 18 ? "adult" : "minor";console.log(status);   // adult
Read it aloud. "Is age at least 18? If yes, adult; otherwise minor."    
When to use. Choosing a value. For executing different actions, use if (Day 6). Nested ternaries are legal but hard to read; limit to one level in this course.
javascript
let marks = 72;let grade = marks >= 90 ? "A" : marks >= 75 ? "B" : "C";   // nested: read right-to-left carefullyconsole.log(grade);   // C
Common mistakes. Forgetting the : branch (SyntaxError); using it for side effects; misreading nested ternaries.
3.8 Operator precedence and associativity
When several operators appear, precedence decides which runs first; associativity decides direction for equal precedence.
Simplified order (high → low) for Phase 1:
Grouping ( )
Postfix x++ x--
Unary ! - + typeof and prefix ++x --x
Exponent ** (right-to-left)
* / %
+ -
< <= > >=
=== !== == !=
&&
||
Ternary ? : (right-to-left)
Assignment = += -= ... (right-to-left)
javascript
console.log(2 + 3 * 4);        
console.log((2 + 3) * 4); 

     // 20console.log(2 ** 3 ** 2);      // 512  (3 ** 2 = 9 first, then 2 ** 9)console.log(10 - 4 - 3);       // 3    (left-to-right)console.log(5 > 3 && 2 > 1);   // true (comparisons before &&)console.log(!true || true);    // true (! first, then ||)
Rule for readable code. Use parentheses whenever two different operator families mix, even if not strictly needed. It costs nothing and removes doubt for the reader and the interviewer.

